INT. ARBITRATION HALL - ANTEROOM - MORNING

A cramped waiting room. Five plastic chairs. A water cooler gurgling.

PATRICIA OYELARAN-WEISS, 40s, litigator, rehearses an opening to the wall. Beside her, her junior, DESHAWN MCALLISTER, 20s, sorts exhibits with shaking hands.

                    PATRICIA
(to Deshawn)
Breathe.

                    DESHAWN
I am.

                    PATRICIA
Out. Not in.

Across the room, ARTHUR KESSLER-BOYD, 60s, opposing counsel, doesn't look up from his phone. His client, LOUISE FAIRCHILD, 50s, grips her purse.

                    LOUISE
(muttering)
It'll be fine, it'll be fine.

The door swings open. A BAILIFF enters.

MARGUERITE DELACROIX, 70s, silver bob, black robe, strides in behind him, and everybody stands.

                    MARGUERITE
(to the room)
Sit. All of you.

They sit. She remains standing. Opens a file.

                    MARGUERITE (CONT'D)
Counsel. Before we begin, someone has been shredding documents.

Arthur's phone slips. Louise stops breathing.
